: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/AUT.png"}] "AUT"]]] [:tr {} [:th {:valign "top"} "Details"] [:td {} "Gates: 34" [:br {}] "Length: 3009 m" [:br {}] "Start Altitude: ?" [:br {}] "Vertical Drop: 832 m"]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "There was no clear favorite, but one thing was certain, the Austrians had the best team. Of the top 20 ranked downhillers, seven were Austrian. The 1976 champion, " [:a {:href "/athletes/88010"} "Franz Klammer"] ", had been the world’s best from 1975-78 but his performances had fallen off, and he was not chosen for Lake Placid. The Austrians chose four skiers with " [:a {:href "/athletes/97861"} "Leonhard Stock"] " picked as an alternate. But his training performances were better than all the other skiers so he was moved up to start in the downhill, dropping " [:a {:href "/athletes/98782"} "Sepp Walcher"] ", the 1978 World Champion, from the start. Stock started ninth, with the lead at that time held by Italy’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/96466"} "Herbert Plank"] ", but Stock bettered his time by 1.63 seconds to take the lead. And it held up, giving him a very surprising gold medal. He had never won a World Cup race, and would not win another international downhill event until 1989. Another Austrian, " [:a {:href "/athletes/98999"} "Peter Wirnsberger"] ", took silver, with " [:a {:href "/athletes/86237"} "Werner Grissmann"] " seventh, and " [:a {:href "/athletes/98877"} "Harti Weirather"] " ninth. The bronze medal went to one of “The Crazy Canucks,” " [:a {:href "/athletes/96491"} "Steve Podborski"] ", the first Canadian man to win an Olympic medal in Alpine skiing."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "Time"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "9"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/97861"} "Leonhard Stock"]] 